= Probability =

== Overview ==
'''Probability''' is a mathematical framework for representing chance, uncertainty, or degrees of support under specified axioms and interpretations.

The probability calculus is mathematically precise, while the meaning assigned to probabilities can vary by interpretation.

== Basic axioms ==
In a standard Kolmogorov framework:

<pre>
P(A) ≥ 0
P(Ω) = 1
P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) for disjoint A and B
</pre>

From these axioms follow many standard probability rules.

== Events and sample space ==
A sample space Ω represents possible outcomes.

Events are subsets of Ω.

A probability measure assigns values between 0 and 1 to suitable events.

== Interpretations ==
Major interpretations include:

* long-run frequency;
* propensity or physical chance;
* subjective degree of belief;
* logical/evidential probability.

The same mathematical calculus can be used under different interpretations.

Therefore:

<pre>
probability ≠ frequency by definition
</pre>

== Conditional probability ==
[[Conditional probability]] represents probability given information or an event.

It is central to dependence, Bayes' theorem, statistical inference, and stochastic modeling.

== Probability and uncertainty ==
Probability is one formal representation of uncertainty.

Not every uncertain question has a justified numerical probability, and assigning a number does not guarantee the model is appropriate.

== Related concepts ==
[[Uncertainty]], [[Evidence]], [[Conditional probability]], and [[Probability distribution]] connect the probability calculus to epistemic use and distributional modeling.

